{red|**This requires courage.** Be honest about what's happening with sexual desire in your marriage, from your perspective.

Consider: • How has desire changed since early in your relationship?}{red| • What factors do you think are affecting desire (yours and your spouse's)?}{red| • What emotions come up when you think about your sexual relationship?}

Write your honest assessment of where things are and how you feel about it:

{red|What turns you on?}{red| What helps desire emerge for you?}

{red|Consider: • What context helps you feel interested in sex?}{red| • What kind of touch or foreplay awakens desire?}{red| • What emotional states make you more open to intimacy?}{red| • What has worked in the past when sex was good?}

List your personal accelerators, be specific:

{red|What shuts you down?}{red| What makes desire disappear or prevents it from emerging?}

{red|Consider: • What stresses or worries kill your desire?}{red| • What relational dynamics make you less interested?}{red| • What physical or body-related factors affect you?}{red| • What mental or emotional states shut you down?}

List your personal brakes, be honest:

{red|Whether you're higher or lower desire, reflect honestly:

What have you been doing that might be making things worse?}

{red|How have you contributed to the pursuit-withdrawal cycle?}

{red|What does your spouse probably need from you that you haven't been giving?}

Write your honest reflection:

{red|**This requires vulnerability.** Be honest with yourself:

How has your physical attraction to your spouse changed over time?}

{red|What factors have contributed to any decrease in attraction?}

{red|What could you focus on that would help attraction grow?}

{red|What effort are you putting (or not putting) into being attractive to your spouse?}

Write your honest assessment:

{red|Explore your relationship with your body:

How do you feel about your body, particularly in intimate contexts?}

{red|What messages have you internalized about what your body should look like?}

{red|How does body shame affect your sexual relationship?}

{red|What would change if you could accept your body as it is?}

Write your honest reflection:

{red|What have you been wanting to communicate to your spouse about your sexual relationship but haven't?}

Consider: • Needs that aren't being met • Things you'd enjoy more (or less) of • What would help you desire sex more • How you feel about your current sexual relationship

Write what you need to say (you can use this to prepare for conversation):

{red|Evaluate the context for intimacy in your marriage:

**Physical environment:** Is your bedroom conducive to intimacy?}{red| What needs to change?}

{red|**Relational climate:** What's the emotional atmosphere usually like before intimate moments?}{red| What would help?}

{red|**Timing:** When do you typically try to be intimate?}{red| Is that optimal?}{red| What timing might work better?}

Write your assessment:

{red|Reflect on your presence during intimate moments:

How present are you typically during sex?}{red| What percentage of you is actually there?}

{red|What most commonly distracts you?}

{red|Do you tend to spectator, observe yourself rather than experience?}

{red|What would help you be more present?}

Write your honest reflection:

{red|Reflect honestly on how your past affects your present intimacy:

What past experiences might be impacting your sexual relationship?}

{red|How do these experiences show up, avoidance, triggers, shame, disconnection?}

{red|What healing has already happened?}{red| What still needs attention?}

{red|Do you need professional help to process these wounds?}

Write your reflection:

{red|What messages have you received about sex, from church, family, culture?}

{red|How have these messages affected your experience of sexual intimacy in marriage?}

{red|What would change if you truly believed sex in marriage is a sacred gift, designed for your pleasure and connection?}

Write your reflection:

{red|Through this journey, you've gained understanding about:

• Why sexual desire fades and how to counteract it • Spontaneous vs responsive desire and how they work • Your personal brakes and accelerators • Navigating desire discrepancy with empathy • Rebuilding physical attraction • Addressing body image and self-consciousness • Communicating about sexual needs • Creating context for desire to flourish • Being present during intimacy • Healing past wounds that affect sexuality • God's design for sexual pleasure in marriage

**This is significant knowledge.** The question is: what will you do with it?}

{red|What's the most important thing you've learned about sexual intimacy through this journey?}

{red|What do you want to remember when things get difficult?}

{red|What has shifted in how you understand yourself, your spouse, or your sexual relationship?}

Write your key insight:

{red|Based on this journey, what specific changes do you need to make?}

Consider: • How you think about desire and sexuality • How you communicate with your spouse about sex • What context you create (or don't create) • How you address your brakes • How you initiate or respond

Write the specific changes you're committing to:
